==================================================
LEARNING OUTCOMES
==================================================

A student completing PONS $101 should be able to:

1. Explain what a blockchain network is.

2. Identify a chain using chain ID.

3. Explain the role of an RPC endpoint.

4. Use a block explorer to inspect a transaction.

5. Explain why a token symbol is not a canonical identity.

6. Identify a token by contract address and chain.

7. Explain a smart-contract factory.

8. Explain a token launch.

9. Explain a bonding curve.

10. Distinguish launch price mechanics from a price peg.

11. Explain trading pairs.

12. Distinguish “paired with” from “pegged to.”

13. Identify base trading fees.

14. Identify creator-related fees.

15. Understand opening-window or snipe-protection mechanics where applicable.

16. Explain why early execution does not automatically prove bot identity.

17. Read a launch event.

18. Read a buy event.

19. Read a sell event.

20. Reconstruct basic launch timing.

21. Calculate launch-to-first-trade latency.

22. Explain behavioral classification.

23. Explain why correlation does not establish identity.

24. Create a research hypothesis.

25. Register an experiment before the outcome.

26. Understand a manifest hash.

27. Understand an unsigned transaction payload.

28. Explain why agents should not automatically sign mainnet transactions.

29. Understand the human release gate.

30. Create an after-action review.

31. Distinguish raw evidence from interpretation.

32. Explain mixed-methods research.

33. Explain reproducibility.

34. Explain creator entitlement.

35. Verify whether assets actually reached a treasury.

36. Explain why public-good intent requires accounting.

37. Read a PONS $101 reproducibility package.

38. Independently reproduce at least one experiment.

